eld line as sent (a repeated header is never read as one of its lines), and the body, which the client takes from the SDK before the SDK reads it and reads itself under the budget and the body-idle and whole-body deadlines. So a body that stalls, is cut, overruns its framing or runs past the budget after a 2xx head is reported beside that 2xx, never in its place. A 2xx is a success only when its body is the one the operation's success carries (none, or one whole document under the root S3 gives it); one whose body is an S3 `<Error>` document instead (S3 sends some failures of CopyObject and CompleteMultipartUpload that way) is reported with that document's code and message beside the 2xx, and any other is unreadable. Listing pages and delete results are read by the same strict rules as an error document, with each `<Size>` plain decimal digits; one that is cut short, followed by anything, or past the budget fails the whole call, never a partial listing or a short delete result. A 2xx ranged or conditional GET streams as a GET object does; both turn off the SDK's response-checksum validation, so they ask for no checksum and a body reaches the client as sent. These verbs read a `Transfer-Encoding` alone, of any coding, on a response to a HEAD or a `304` to a GET as RFC 9112 §6.1 allows, where no body follows: it is reported in the head with no refusal beside it, as a `Content-Length` on a `304` to a conditional GET is, and any byte after the head is still an overrun. A probe that got no answer is reported as the older verbs report it: the connect or the operation deadline as its typed timeout, any other loss before a response head as `NoResponse`, and neither as a request not built. The bucket probes send a bucket name as given: one request to the `--endpoint`, whether it is an IP address or a host name, at the endpoint's path, `/`, and the name as one path segment, percent-encoded where a byte is not unreserved. The client checks no name against S3's naming rules, so